  "prediction": "**Aegis Ballistic Missile Defense (BMD) Program: Background and Issues for Congress**\n\nThe Aegis BMD program is a critical component of the United States' ballistic missile defense (BMD) capabilities, providing a capability for conducting BMD operations on Navy Aegis cruisers and destroyers. The program is carried out by the Missile Defense Agency (MDA) and the Navy, with funding provided through MDA's budget and the Navy's budget. The Aegis BMD system has undergone significant upgrades and improvements over the years, with the latest variants capable of intercepting intercontinental ballistic missiles (ICBMs).\n\nThe Aegis BMD system uses the Standard Missile-3 (SM-3) interceptor, which is designed to intercept ballistic missiles above the atmosphere (exo-atmospheric intercept). The SM-3 has undergone several upgrades, including the SM-3 Block IIA, which has a larger diameter and more room for rocket fuel, allowing it to have a longer range and a larger-diameter kinetic warhead. The SM-3 Block IIA has been tested successfully in flight tests, with a combined total of 40 successful intercepts in 49 attempts.\n\nThe Aegis BMD program has several variants, including the 3.6.X, 4.0.3, 4.1, 5.0 CU, 5.1, and 6.X variants. The latest variant, the 6.X, is also known as the Aegis Baseline 10 variant. The Aegis BMD system has been deployed on several Aegis ships, including the Ticonderoga (CG-47) class cruisers and the Arleigh Burke (DDG-51) class destroyers.\n\nThe European Phased Adaptive Approach (EPAA) is a regional BMD operation that uses Aegis BMD ships, a land-based radar in Europe, and two Aegis Ashore sites in Romania and Poland to defend Europe against ballistic missile threats from countries such as Iran. The EPAA has several phases, with Phase 1 involving the deployment of Aegis BMD ships and a land-based radar in Europe, Phase 2 involving the establishment of an Aegis Ashore site in Romania with SM-3 IB interceptors, and Phase 3 involving the establishment of an Aegis Ashore site in Poland with SM-3 IIA interceptors.\n\nThe Aegis BMD program has several issues that need to be addressed, including the burden that BMD operations may be placing on the Navy's fleet",
